@iSazonov

In this case I was looking at a memory profile and deduced it through circumstantial evidence.  Then used more runtime inspection to see that the hash codes were different for seemingly empty objects (of type PSMethodInvocationConstraints) where those PSMethodInvocationConstraints were used as a tuple elements which was a cache key.

There are certainly all kinds of ways to measure at runtime, for example by adding telemetry metrics.  Microsoft seems to have at least 10 different ways they measure stuff like this :)

review_comments:
Btw I'm not at all beholden to the implementation in the submitted fix. Because it generates a hash code itself to pass into a method that expects an object to be hashed.

I've implemented a HashCode class myself similar to yours but included methods for AddElements(ICollection<T>) to make it easier to deal with collection elements.

Also have recently realized that Array is IStructuralEquatable, so when using array as a tuple element for a dictionary key, I'd wrap in a class like this:

```
    class StructuralEquatableWrapper(IStructuralEquatable target)
    {
      public IStructuralEquatable Target => target;
      public override int GetHashCode() => target.GetHashCode(EqualityComparer<object>.Default);
      public override bool Equals(object? obj) => obj is StructuralEquatableWrapper wrapper && target.Equals(wrapper.Target, EqualityComparer<object>.Default);
    }
```

But I do maintain the issue is in urgent need of a fix.
